	doi = {10.1002/9781118706992.ch11},
	abstract = {This chapter describes principal methods of storing hydrogen: compression in gas cylinders, as a cryogenic liquid, as a reversible metal hydride, and as a metal hydride that reacts with water. Compressed hydrogen storage is most widely employed in places where there is moderate but variable demand. Safety considerations must feature strongly in the design of any fuel-cell system. The container or tank for storing cryogenic liquid hydrogen is a large, strongly reinforced vacuum (Dewar) flask. Proponents of hydrogen storage via a reversible metal hydride note that it is safer than a pressurized cylinder that could rapidly and dangerously discharge in the event of failure or damage. Irreversible chemical hydrides that react with water may find some success, but manufacturing and recycle costs need to be reduced dramatically. The so-called ‘nanostructured’ materials continue tube evaluated for hydrogen storage and other catalytic properties.},
